welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and our guest  is Frances rosenbluth deputy provost for  the social sciences and faculty  development at yale university professor  Rosenbluth is a comparative political  economist with research interests in war  and constitutions Japanese politics and  political economy and the political  economy of gender the author of numerous  articles and book chapters professor  Rosenbluth has written several books the  politics of oligarchy institutional  choice in Imperial Japan Japan's  political marketplace financial politics  in contemporary Japan and Japan  transformed political change and  economic reform today will speak with  her about her newest book women work and  power the political economy of gender  inequality welcome professor rosenbluth  thanks for having me so what is the  premise of your book this book is about  the political economy of gender and what  we attempt to do in this book is to  excavate reasons for why women have  lower status than men in politics  economics and society and it's it's  common to think about this problem from  a number of angles for example exploring  the reasons behind gender stereotypes  and unfavorable views of women but in  this book we are putting aside for now  the unconscious bias that people may  have towards women and focusing instead  on structural reasons in the economy and  in politics for why women do not achieve  the same levels of prominence as men ok  so why hold aside the bias and  stereotypes well we're not arguing that  these things don't matter that they  don't exist but we are trying to explain  why they have come to be the way they  are so in a sense we're trying to  understand why gender norms are the way  they are why are women view too  currently from men and so we do that  with the standard tools of political  economy okay and what are those tools  and how can they help explain why  patriarchy has been so prevalent  throughout the world for so long the  standard tools of political economy are  begin with basic micro economic analysis  of the marketplace and in particular we  focus on labor markets we try to  understand why efficiency concerns  efficiency considerations by  corporations by employers may lead them  to avoid hiring and promoting women at  the same rate as men and how that in  turn leads to a lower demand for female  labor than male labor which translates  into weaker bargaining power by women in  their family unit where the female is  earning less money than the male and  therefore has to put more emphasis as  well as effort into keeping the family  going as a unit because she can't rely  on her own remunerative capacity her own  money making capability she really needs  a male patron to ensure her livelihood  okay and in doing your research what  countries have you looked at this book  is comparative it focuses mostly on the  developed world rich democracies but we  also at the beginning of the boat take  it all the way back to prehistoric times  to give a very long overview of females  at status of females relative to males  in human society and you start actually  with the hunter-gatherers and things  seem to have been a little bit different  back then than they are today right so  the the data we have for understanding  hunter-gatherer societies is of course  very imperfect incomplete so we're  relying primarily on physical  anthropology  who have uncovered various kinds of data  including the size of bones or artifacts  as well as anthropologists who study  hunter-gatherer societies today these  are not perfect examples of  hunter-gatherer societies as they must  have existed because they have been  contaminated I suppose you could say by  their contact with modern developed  societies but hunter-gatherer societies  as far as we can say we're more gender  egalitarianism the reason we suggest is  because females in those societies were  able to raise their own food sources  they weren't hunters primarily they were  there were gatherers but the food they  gathered the legumes the you know fruits  and berries whatever they were  collecting from their environments for  their families seem to have accounted  for almost four-fifths of the caloric  intake of those early societies and the  fact that they were able to provide  their own food and those for their  children if necessary in the absence of  a male patron made it possible for them  to consider the possibility of leaving a  unsatisfactory relationship with a male  ok so here we are today why do you think  that men have dominated the world for so  very long we focus on the change in  relationships between men and women that  occurred principally with the onset of  sedentary agriculture and what the  establishment of fixed communities did  was to put a premium on male brawn the  ability of males to till intensively the  land often with the help of livestock  and to protect the food or the community  that was fixed in a position and to  protect the stored assets of the  community so this led to a division of  labor within societies within  families where the male specialized in  the production of food female  specialized in the care of children and  any kind of work they could do that was  consistent with continued care of  children this isn't to say that females  don't also engage in agriculture and I'm  sure you know that in the developing  world women do a lot of agriculture so  male Braun did not push women out of  agriculture all together but when the  work that women do is part of a family  unit a family enterprise a family farm  she is not mobile she cannot take her  investment in those skills and move them  to another family because she is really  rooted her assets are in that family ok  so do politics and government policies  influence the gender gap certainly  governments can do a lot to help us wage  that the problems that females face in  weak bargaining with in modern society  but some of the policies that  governments enact to try to help women  have unintended consequences that may  not be helpful so for example here's  here's something that was paradoxical to  me as I was studying this problem you  think about the welfare states in Europe  and the fact that they protect labor  they raised the floor under under wages  and given that women tend to be  clustered at the bottom you would think  that this would be a very female or  gender friendly thing to do it has the  paradoxical effect of increasing the  cost to an employer of hiring a woman if  a woman is nonetheless more likely to  interrupt her career to take care of  family and to do family work so the fact  that a company has to have a long term  contract with a woman has to hire her at  a certain minimum  rate of salary means that they are  required to pay a woman more than her  productivity or her contribution to the  company may warrant if the cost to the  company of her career interruption is is  less than the cost to a company of a  man's employment which is continuous and  uninterruptedly and if we if we look at  today since women and men are arguably  equally equipped in the labor market do  you think male domination in the United  States is now the result of a cultural  phenomenon perpetuated by this very long  history of it being that way I think  that there is a lot of cultural inertia  and so although we are no longer in a an  agricultural society a lot of the norms  that we espouse unconsciously are left  over from an earlier period so I do  think that's true to a certain extent  but I also think that norms gender norms  in the United States and elsewhere have  changed quite a lot so that we can  expect better treatment now than our  ancestors even a few generations ago  sure sure so why study gender inequality  well I think although we've come a long  way there's still quite a ways to go so  it's partly just to try to unpack this  puzzle of why despite the fact that we  espouse these these goals of equality  it's so hard for us to to have real  equality and this book is an attempt to  make an inroad into that question okay  wonderful and I can't help but conclude  the interview today by asking you do you  think at any point in the future that  women would rule the world rule the  world probably not but be more equal  certainly I hope so  okay I'm wonderful thank you so much for  being here with us today and sharing  some of your research thank you very  much for having me for more information  about professor Rosen blue and her work  please visit our website at yale edu  backslash macmillan report be sure to  join us again for another episode of the  macmillan report made possible through  funding from the Whitney and Betty  Macmillan Center for International and  area studies at Yale  you
 

